.Dd April 2, 2026
.Dt VIEW 1
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm view
.Nd start vi in readonly mode
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl sSv
.Op Fl c Ar command
.Op Fl t Ar tag
.Op Ar +command
.Op Ar file
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility is the readonly entry point for
.Xr vi 1 .
It is implemented through the shared
.Pa usr.lib/exvi
frontend parser by recognizing
.Nm
as an invocation alias for
.Ql vi\ -R .
.Pp
All normal visual editing, motion, search, tag, and prompt behavior remains
available, but plain writes are rejected unless forced with
.Ql \&! .
.Pp
This entry point exists so users can inspect files without accidentally
overwriting them.
The active status line shows
.Ql [Readonly]
while the session remains readonly.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Nm
accepts the same options and startup command forms as
.Xr vi 1 ,
except that readonly mode is enabled implicitly.
.Pp
The following forms are commonly used:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl c Ar command
Execute
.Ar command
after startup processing.
.It Fl t Ar tag
Start by editing the file and location named by
.Ar tag .
.It Ar +command
Execute
.Ar command
after startup processing.
.It Ar file
Open
.Ar file
as the current buffer.
If multiple files are given, they become the initial argument list.
.El
.Sh NOTES
Readonly mode may be cleared inside the editor with
.Ic :set noreadonly ,
or bypassed for a single write with force forms such as
.Ql :w!
and
.Ql :wq! .
.Pp
Substrate does not currently provide separate
.Nm
startup or recovery companion commands.
Startup-file loading and recovery behavior are documented in
.Xr vi 1
and
.Xr ex 1 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ex 1 ,
.Xr vi 1
